WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.849 --> 00:00:05.359
Critics are calling "The Batman" the best
DC movie in years, but how does it stack up

00:00:05.359 --> 00:00:07.270
against the MCU?

00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:11.450
Keep watching for how it beats most Marvel
movies at the superhero movie game.

00:00:11.450 --> 00:00:12.831
Warning: Some spoilers ahead!

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:19.200
With a few noteworthy exceptions, like Wakanda
in "Black Panther" and some of its intergalactic

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:24.050
locales, the MCU generally doesn't place a
huge emphasis on its settings.

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:28.360
This is in part because the MCU is essentially
the same as our world, just with super-beings.

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:30.369
"Mr. Musk, how are you?"

00:00:30.369 --> 00:00:31.369
"Hi, Pepper!

00:00:31.369 --> 00:00:32.820
Congratulations on the promotion."

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:34.219
"Thank you very much."

00:00:34.219 --> 00:00:38.760
This is a fine strategy for making its stories
feel real without spending a lot of time on

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:42.690
world-building, allowing viewers to fill in
the blanks based on their own experiences.

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:47.170
Christopher Nolan's "Dark Knight" trilogy
utilized this strategy as well, simply treating

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:51.829
Gotham as if it were Chicago, New York, or
whatever real city the movies were filming

00:00:51.829 --> 00:00:52.829
in.

00:00:52.829 --> 00:00:55.649
But it's world-building where "The Batman"
excels most of all.

00:00:55.649 --> 00:01:00.410
Its Gotham is a marvel of production design,
as gothic as Tim Burton's version but less

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:01.469
cartoony.

00:01:01.469 --> 00:01:06.070
A lot of thought was put into not just how
this distinctive city looks, but how it feels,

00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:10.049
what it's like to live there, and why this
corrupted place needs Batman in the first

00:01:10.049 --> 00:01:11.049
place.

00:01:11.049 --> 00:01:15.100
The sense of place in "The Batman" not only
exceeds the majority of the MCU, but much

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:17.440
of the superhero movie genre as a whole.

00:01:18.262 --> 00:01:22.200
The MCU mostly sticks to a formula, with minor
variations.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:26.620
Marvel might talk up "Captain America: The
Winter Soldier" as a political thriller, "Ant-Man"

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:31.950
as a heist movie, "Eternals" as a philosophical
drama, and so on — but despite these different

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:36.259
flavors, these movies generally end up in
the same place, as action movies building

00:01:36.259 --> 00:01:39.009
toward a CGI-filled third act battle.

00:01:39.009 --> 00:01:43.340
When Warner Bros. hyped "The Batman" as a
neo-noir detective film, however, the studio

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:45.150
actually gave us what they were selling.

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:49.140
There are still the action scenes you expect
from a superhero movie, but there are far

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:51.579
fewer of them than most of its contemporaries.

00:01:51.579 --> 00:01:56.189
The vast majority of the three-hour runtime
is spent investigating crime scenes, interrogating

00:01:56.189 --> 00:01:59.659
suspects, and struggling to uncover conspiracies.

00:01:59.659 --> 00:02:03.200
Critics aren't kidding when they've compared
it to the crime thrillers of David Fincher;

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:07.319
it has more in common structurally with a
film like "Se7en" or "Zodiac" than with "The

00:02:07.319 --> 00:02:09.229
Avengers" or even "The Dark Knight."

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:12.420
"Oh you're really not as smart as I thought
you were."

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:17.530
Though the Marvel films have their moments
of beauty, the studio's insistence on a consistent

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:21.420
house style has resulted in some pretty bland
visuals.

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:25.070
Even when the technical skill on display in
these movies is impressive, critics say the

00:02:25.070 --> 00:02:29.190
general aesthetic is washed-out, and sometimes
flat-out ugly.

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:32.970
This is absolutely not the case with "The
Batman," which ranks among the most visually

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:35.870
stunning blockbusters in recent memory.

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:40.720
Shot by Greig Fraser, the cinematographer
who earned Oscar nominations for 2016's "Lion"

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:46.040
and 2021's "Dune," "The Batman" is a cinematographic
achievement that deserved to be remembered

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:47.660
come awards season.

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:52.370
Filming a movie set almost entirely at night
and in the shadows is a challenge, but Fraser

00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:55.960
keeps the images clear and beautiful even
when the darkness is overwhelming.

00:02:55.960 --> 00:03:01.040
"The Batman" is particularly inventive with
light sources, using everything from gunfire

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:04.930
to the engines of the Batmobile as substitutes
for traditional lighting.

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:09.990
This might not be the most even grounds for
comparison, because to-date, Marvel movies

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:12.050
generally aren't trying to be scary.

00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:16.800
"Dr. Strange in the Multiverse of Madness,"
directed by Sam Raimi, might change all that.

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:21.180
As such, it's no slight on the MCU to say
that "The Batman" is significantly scarier

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:26.130
than anything the MCU has attempted thus far,
and it is yet another thing Matt Reeves' film

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:27.150
excels at.

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:31.790
The Riddler's grotesque killings push the
limits of the PG-13 rating to the extreme.

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:36.430
If anything, the restraint the film shows
to avoid R-rated content makes what's implied

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:38.060
all the more disturbing.

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:42.320
From ticking bombs to topical political nightmares
to one of the most startlingly believable

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:46.850
depictions of near-future climate disaster,
there are multiple sequences in "The Batman"

00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:48.360
that could haunt your nightmares.

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:51.010
Needless to say, think twice before letting
the kids watch.

00:03:51.868 --> 00:03:56.370
This is also something of an apples-to-oranges
comparison, because the MCU's special effects

00:03:56.370 --> 00:04:01.540
spend a lot of time depicting things significantly
less "real" than anything in "The Batman."

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:07.040
Even with that in mind, however, Marvel Studios
can be inconsistent regarding special effects.

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:10.760
Some stuff looks great, but there are many
questionable moments of rushed or lower-quality

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:13.510
effects in otherwise high quality movies.

00:04:13.510 --> 00:04:18.130
In terms of consistent realism, "The Batman"
beats basically every Marvel movie when it

00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:19.850
comes to special effects.

00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:23.460
Some of the "special effects" in "The Batman"
look real because they are real.

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:26.680
The Batmobile driving through fire is a real
practical stunt.

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:31.440
However, there's also a lot of CGI used so
seamlessly in the film that most viewers won't

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:34.180
even be aware certain shots are effects shots.

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:39.350
"The Batman" makes extensive use of ILM's
StageCraft system, previously used on "The

00:04:39.350 --> 00:04:43.600
Mandalorian," which combines projections with
virtual reality technology to allow for the

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:46.419
most realistic cinematography of CGI backgrounds.

00:04:47.132 --> 00:04:50.760
Let's be fair and acknowledge that Marvel
Studios has gotten a lot better at villains

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:52.770
from Phase 3 of the MCU onward.

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:58.030
Vulture, Killmonger, and Thanos are all stand-out
antagonists and among the most interesting

00:04:58.030 --> 00:04:59.790
parts of their respective movies.

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:04.560
In its first two phases, however, the MCU
had a major villain problem, with no one aside

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:09.790
from Loki and Ultron providing much in the
way of entertainment or interesting personalities.

00:05:09.790 --> 00:05:14.100
The focus was on developing a wide range of
heroes, with their enemies being an afterthought.

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:18.810
"The Batman" has three major villains — The
Riddler, The Penguin, and Carmine Falcone

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:23.850
— and they're all superior to the non-Loki
villains of the MCU Phases 1 and 2.

00:05:23.850 --> 00:05:24.850
"Whoa!

00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:26.650
Take it easy, sweetheart."

00:05:26.650 --> 00:05:30.720
Paul Dano's Riddler is the most significant
villain in moving the plot along, and he's

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:34.850
also the scariest — but he still has complex
motives for why he's committing his horrible

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:35.850
crimes.

00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:39.760
Colin Farrell's Penguin is a fun gangster
character, ready for his spinoff show, and

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:44.110
John Turturro's Falcone oozes menace as the
villain with the most personal connection

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:45.110
to the Wayne family.

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:50.380
The MCU is exceptional at what it does in
terms of building an interconnected franchise

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:53.350
where audiences feel they have to see everything.

00:05:53.350 --> 00:05:57.760
Even the films that do work as stand-alone
stories are designed with call-backs and future

00:05:57.760 --> 00:05:59.060
teases in mind.

00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:03.530
Between the epic conclusion of the Infinity
Saga and the multiverse adventures in Phase

00:06:03.530 --> 00:06:07.900
4, the MCU has only grown more confident in
its viewers' familiarity with past movies

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:12.050
— and in the case of "Spider-Man: No Way
Home," movies that weren't even part of the

00:06:12.050 --> 00:06:13.090
MCU.

00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:17.460
This is all well and good, but we can't dismiss
the value of a well-told self-contained story

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:21.400
that doesn't feel like it's putting off any
important narrative or character development

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:22.400
for sequels.

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:27.110
"The Batman" might be the start of a "cinematic
universe" of its own, with sequels and HBO

00:06:27.110 --> 00:06:30.900
Max spinoffs on the way, but it's successful
as a complete story.

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:35.040
Only one particular scene feels a bit too
Marvel-esque in terms of playing like a blatant

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:39.880
sequel tease — but director Matt Reeves
told Variety that's not actually the case.

